<section xml:id="sec-additional-resources">
    <title>Additional Resources</title>
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-textbooks">
        <title>Textbooks</title>
        <p>
            This course utilizes <em>Openstax Calculus: Volume 2</em> (Chapters 5 and 6) and <em>OPenstax Calculus: Volume </em> (Chapters 2 to 5) both by Gilbert Strang
            and Edwin "Jed" Herman, et. al. Both text can be read online for free and downloaded as a PDF for free from <url href="https://openstax.org/" visual="openstax.org"/>
        </p>
        <p>
            Another excellent resource are <url href="https://tutorial.math.lamar.edu/" visual="Paul's Online Math Notes"/> by Paul Davies at Lamar University. These notes cover math from algebra to differential 
            equations and are great if you need a quick review of a topic. I have been using them since I was in college! Thankfully, Professor Davies has been updating and improving his notes
            since 2003.
        </p>
    </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-youtube-videos">
        <title>YouTube Videos</title>
        <p>
            Here is a list of some YouTube channels with content featured in these notes plus other channels with lecture-length videos.
        </p>
        <p>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Organic Chemistry Tutor</title>
                <p>
                    The master of STEM videos! Check out there extensive <url href="GiCojsAWRj0&amp;list=PL0o_zxa4K1BWYThyV4T2Allw6zY0jEumv" visual ="calculus playlist"/>
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Professor Leonard</title>
                <p>
                   Full length (90 to 150 minute) lecture videos from prealgebra to differential equations plus statistics! Check out his <url href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FoNLQvf4NUs&amp;list=PLDesaqWTN6EQ2J4vgsN1HyBeRADEh4Cw-&amp;index=14"
                   visual = "Calculus 2 Playlist"/> and <url href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tGVnBAHLApA&amp;list=PLDesaqWTN6ESk16YRmzuJ8f6-rnuy0Ry7" visual="Calculus 3 Playlist"/>
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Professor Dave Explains</title>
                <p>
                   He explains many things in STEM in short videos. Here is his <url href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rBVi_9qAKTU&amp;list=PLybg94GvOJ9ELZEe9s2NXTKr41Yedbw7M" visual="Calculus Playlist"/> 
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>3Blue1Brown</title>
                <p>
                 Mathematician Grant Sanderson's chanel. Here is his <url href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WUvTyaaNkzM&amp;list=PLZHQObOWTQDMsr9K-rj53DwVRMYO3t5Yr"
                 visual = "Essence of Calculus Playlist"/>     
                </p>
            </li>
            
            <li>
                <title>Krista King</title>
                <p>
                A math teacher explains it all in short example videos. Here are her <url href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_nNLLjOQs0&amp;list=PLJ8OrXpbC-BMObozItpbiZ8f2pjf3qS9M&amp;index=155"
                visual ="Calculus II"/> and <url href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CxtgMNAQr8g&amp;list=PLJ8OrXpbC-BMdeuQfJDVRJ5DPMduSzVow" visual ="Calculus III"/> playlists.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>JK Math</title>
                <p>
                 I discovered this channel while writing these notes. The <url href="https://www.youtube.com/@JKMath/playlists" visual="JK Mathematics"/> channel is loaded with 
                 simple and clear tutorials.  
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Math is Power 4U</title>
                <p>
                  If you have ever watched a WAMAP question video, you have seen video on this <url href="https://www.youtube.com/@Mathispower4u" visual="channel"/>
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Nancy Pi</title>
                <p>
                 A great <url href="https://www.youtube.com/@NancyPi/playlists" visual = "channel"/> with review videos for algebra and calculus.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Dr. Trefor Bazett</title>
                <p>
                 Canadian mathematician's <url href="https://www.youtube.com/@DrTrefor/courses" visual="channel"/> which covers every math class at Green River.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Just for Fun</title>
                
                <p>
                     Brady Haran's <url href="https://www.numberphile.com/" visual="Numberphile"/> is the coolest math site on the internet. Here is the <url href="https://www.youtube.com/numberphile" visual = "Numberphile YouTube channel"/>.
    The funniest math related YouTube channel is Matt Parker's <url href="https://www.youtube.com/@standupmaths" visual="Stand-Up Maths Channel"/>. Also check out Matt's podcast called
    <em>A Problem Squared</em>. And also check out Matt on <url href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=65r_1TzJXaQ" visual= "Adam Savage's Tested"/>. 
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </p>


    </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-calculators">
        <title>Calculators</title>
        
    
        <p>
            These notes include a pop-up GeoGebra-3D calculator. If you click on the calculator icon on the top of each page, then a GeoGebra-3D calculator will pop-up 
            in the lower right of the screen. You can find more about the free GeoGebra calculators at <url href="https://www.geogebra.org/"/>.
        </p>
        <p>
            For a regular graphing calculator, you may use a TI-84 graphing calculator, the Desmos calculator or I recommend the Numworks calculator, which is available as 
            a free iPhone or Android app! Or you can use the embedded emulator below.
        </p>
    
    <figure>
        <caption>The Numworks Calculator</caption>
        <interactive xml:id="numworks" iframe ="https://www.numworks.com/simulator/embed/" width="368px" height="720px"/>
    </figure> 
</subsection>
</section>